{"type":"message","id":"06b71e22","parentId":"217d7a86","timestamp":"2026-05-18T07:36:30.811Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ssPZq8c0nQZbKyAWqsWA0or7","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** You MUST naturally embed 2-3 markdown links inside the body text. These links MUST point to the exact source URLs provided in your RESEARCH_JSON. Example: `[according to recent data from Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/...)`\n- **CHART SOURCES:** The orchestrator will provide the charts. Simply embed exactly 2 mentions of the `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]` tag inside the H2 sections.\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** \n  - Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words)\n  - Primary keyword in first 100 words (opening hook)\n  - Primary keyword in at least one H2 and the Conclusion\n  - Secondary keywords must appear in H2 headings and their body paragraphs.\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I have the primary and secondary keywords.\n- [ ] I have the source URLs from the `RESEARCH_JSON` input.\n- [ ] I will write EXACTLY in this order: META → Hook → H2#1 → H2#2 → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n- [ ] I will write NOTHING outside this order.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nOutput this exact block at the very top. No conversational filler before it. The very first word must be \"META\".\n- SEO Title: [50–60 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: Primary Keyword + Brand Name + Value]\n- Meta Description: [150–160 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: What the article offers + Unique angle + Call to Action]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase-hyphens-only, e.g. bitcoin-price-hits-new-all-time-high]\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [e.g. \"Bitcoin price drop\"]\n- Secondary Keywords: [e.g. \"BTC resistance\", \"crypto market pullback\", \"$80K Bitcoin\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 Title — must contain primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(Do NOT add an H2 heading above these paragraphs)*\nPara 1: State the core event with a specific price or data point. 2–3 sentences. Start with the primary keyword naturally. Be direct.\nPara 2: Market reaction or supporting data. One additional observation (volume, altcoins, funding rates). 2–3 sentences.\nPara 3: One-line reframe that sets the article's tone. (\"This isn't panic. It looks like traders taking money off the table after a strong run.\")\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3 (REQUIRED): FIRST H2 SECTION\n**## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]**\n\n[1–2 intro paragraphs explaining the news at a high level.]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 1: e.g. \"Profit-Taking Shows Up at Resistance\"]**\n[1–2 short paragraphs]\n[Optional 3-item bullet list]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 2: e.g. \"Altcoins Start Losing Steam\"]**\n[2–3 very short paragraphs — 1 to 3 sentences each]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 4 (REQUIRED): SECOND H2 SECTION\n**## Key [Levels / Data Signals / Numbers] Traders Should Watch [Now / Next]**\n\n[1 intro paragraph]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 1: e.g. \"$75K Needs to Hold\"]**\n[1 paragraph — what this level means, what happens if it holds/breaks]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 2: e.g. \"$80K Remains the Barrier\"]**\n[1 paragraph]\n[Required 3-item bullet list starting with \"Look for:\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 5 (REQUIRED): CONCLUSION\n**## Conclusion**\n\nPara 1: Restate the core event in one sentence using the primary keyword. Name the key level or data point.\nPara 2: What it means for the near-term structure. One or two clean sentences.\nPara 3: Actionable close for the reader. What they should do or watch. End on a direct, confident note.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 6 (REQUIRED): FAQS\n**## FAQs**\n\n**1. Why did [primary event] happen?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**3. What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**4. How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 7 (REQUIRED): SOURCES FOOTER\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n\nSTOP GENERATING HERE. DO NOT ADD ANY BROADER CONTEXT SECTIONS. \nState your final word count on the last line of your output as: [Word Count: XXXX]\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\nBefore finalizing your generation, mentally verify:\n[ ] Exactly 1 H1, 2 H2s, 4 H3s, Conclusion, FAQs, Sources?\n[ ] No em-dashes anywhere?\n[ ] Primary keyword in H1 first 5 words?\n[ ] 2-3 anchor links embedded in body text pointing to source URLs?\n[ ] Word count stated on final line?\n[ ] No sections added beyond the 7 defined above?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779089790811}}
{"type":"message","id":"f71ef15b","parentId":"06b71e22","timestamp":"2026-05-18T07:37:54.399Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_LA8gyfnv3G9bn1GdqObLruSB","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\ntext = '''<thinking>\n- [x] I have the primary and secondary keywords.\n- [x] I have the source URLs from the RESEARCH_JSON input.\n- [x] I will write EXACTLY in this order: META → Hook → H2#1 → H2#2 → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n- [x] I will write NOTHING outside this order.\n- Target length: about 1120 words.\n</thinking>\n\nMETA\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin Selloff: Coinography Analysis of BTC Risks\n- Meta Description: Track the bitcoin selloff below $77K, key BTC support levels, and the macro triggers behind today's drop. Read the risks and levels traders should watch now.\n- URL Slug: bitcoin-selloff-below-77000-oil-yields\n- Categories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: bitcoin selloff\n- Secondary Keywords: crypto market pullback, BTC support levels\n\n# Bitcoin Selloff Sends BTC Below $77,000 as Macro Pressure Builds\n\nBitcoin selloff conditions intensified on May 18 as BTC slipped to about $76,900, breaking below the $77,000 mark during a broad risk-off move. The drop put the market back at a two-week low and reopened the debate over whether May could produce a deeper correction.\n\nThe weakness was not isolated to BTC. [CoinDesk’s market coverage](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-under-usd77-000-as-oil-shock-and-treasury-yields-hit-risk-ass) tied the move to an oil shock and higher Treasury yields, while separate reporting pointed to more than $563 million in crypto liquidations as traders got caught on the wrong side of the move. SOL, DOGE, and XRP also fell around 5%, reinforcing that this was a market-wide reset rather than a single-asset problem.\n\nThis is not panic. It looks like traders cutting risk after a strong rebound while macro pressure starts doing the heavy lifting.\n\n## Crypto market pullback: What this bitcoin selloff shows\n\nThe current crypto market pullback is being framed as more than routine volatility. [According to CoinDesk’s feed](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-under-usd77-000-as-oil-shock-and-treasury-yields-hit-risk-ass), the immediate catalyst was a mix of oil-driven inflation worries and rising Treasury yields, two forces that usually hit speculative assets first.\n\nThat macro backdrop matters because Bitcoin had already been consolidating after a 40% advance. When price is stretched and leveraged positioning is crowded, even a modest external shock can push BTC through short-term support and trigger liquidations across majors.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### Sell in May fears meet real macro stress\n\nThe seasonal debate is getting louder because 2026 lines up with prior midterm election bear phases. CoinTelegraph noted that Bitcoin fell from nearly $10,000 to about $7,000 in May 2018, then dropped from roughly $40,000 to $28,500 in May 2022 before sliding to $20,000 in June.\n\nBut [CoinTelegraph’s analyst roundup](https://cointelegraph.com/news/sell-in-may-historical-pattern-could-see-btc-back-at-33k-analyst?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) also made the cleaner point: the calendar did not cause those crashes. Jeff Ko tied the earlier drawdowns to specific shocks such as the Mt. Gox aftermath, China’s ICO crackdown, Fed tightening, and later the Terra and FTX failures.\n\n- 2018 came with post-Mt. Gox stress and China policy pressure\n- 2022 came with aggressive Fed tightening and crypto-specific blowups\n- 2026 so far looks more like a macro-led risk reduction than a systemic breakdown\n\n### Why the structure still looks different this cycle\n\nKo argued that repeating a 70% to 80% collapse from here is a high bar. Spot ETFs, corporate treasury demand, and progress around the CLARITY Act have widened the buyer base compared with earlier bear markets.\n\nThat does not rule out more downside. Ko said a move into the mid-$60,000s or even high-$50,000s could still be defensible if a macro shock intensifies or ETF outflows start cascading.\n\nThe key distinction is scale. A washout toward $33,000 would likely need something systemic to break, not just a familiar sell in May narrative.\n\n## Key BTC support levels traders should watch now\n\nFor traders, the debate is less about seasonal slogans and more about whether key BTC support levels can absorb this latest wave of selling. [Google News aggregation](https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixAFBVV95cUxQajIxZExWVjJnMXVteHdZVGVZZFNzbzlvZXVNSFFLaWR2Mm5HekNyVmxrUXpTX0lMMnJfMkJmV1FGdlcwN0U0YVF3U3ZUU2x0ZGhnSG1icE9aM0VSQkJVQldVYnpMN1I1YlhIUXVZTUlTd2NZN2h6VjZieUx5LTFwV0FtRVZQREFCVEZFWTYtU182TFZDTERFcmcySVZqRF9GalRBbXJTR0h2WVVnaHdJa2E2a0dNZ1dURnlpbVpxWERLZkI1?oc=5) showed the same theme across outlets, from broader altcoin weakness to a Bloomberg report that crypto liquidations topped half a billion dollars.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### $76,000 needs to hold\n\nMichaël van de Poppe flagged the $76,000 zone as the level currently preventing a larger breakdown. If buyers defend it, BTC can stay in a post-rally consolidation range. If that floor gives way on strong volume, traders should expect a move toward lower boundaries and a faster test of downside liquidity.\n\n### $78,000 remains the short-term reclaim\n\nOn the upside, getting back above $78,000 would help cool the immediate bearish tone because several analysts see sub-$78,000 trading as the point where bears start regaining control. A reclaim would not erase the bitcoin selloff, but it would suggest the move was more flush than trend reversal.\n\n- Look for: ETF flow data and whether outflows accelerate after the break below $77,000\n- Look for: Treasury yield and oil moves, since both are driving risk sentiment across markets\n- Look for: Liquidation totals and altcoin relative weakness, which often signal whether forced selling is ending\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe bitcoin selloff has pushed BTC under $77,000, with $76,000 now acting as the line traders cannot afford to ignore.\n\nNear-term structure still points to a correction inside a broader range, not proof of a full cycle collapse. That changes quickly if macro pressure deepens and ETF flows turn decisively negative.\n\nWatch the $76,000 support, the $78,000 reclaim zone, and cross-market signals from yields and oil. If those inputs stabilize, BTC can base. If they worsen, stay defensive and respect the trend.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did bitcoin selloff happen?**\nBitcoin fell as a macro risk-off move hit speculative assets. CoinDesk tied the drop to an oil shock and higher Treasury yields, while liquidations amplified the move after BTC slipped through short-term support. Once price moved into the $76,000 to $77,000 zone, leveraged traders across crypto started getting forced out.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger correction?**\nIt could become one, but the current setup does not guarantee a full breakdown. Analysts such as Michaël van de Poppe said the chart still looks like consolidation after a 40% run, not an obvious call for fresh cycle lows. The bigger warning sign would be a clean loss of $76,000 with worsening macro conditions.\n\n**3. What levels or signals should traders watch now?**\nThe first level is $76,000, which is the nearby support zone analysts are tracking. The next is $78,000, because reclaiming it would weaken the immediate bearish case. Beyond price, watch ETF flows, liquidation totals, and whether oil and Treasury yields keep tightening financial conditions for risk assets.\n\n**4. How does this affect the broader crypto market?**\nBitcoin rarely drops alone during a macro-driven move, and this session was no exception. CoinDesk-linked coverage showed SOL, DOGE, and XRP also down about 5%, while Bloomberg’s feed highlighted more than half a billion dollars in liquidations across crypto. That kind of selling usually pressures high-beta altcoins harder than BTC.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\nThe sell in May debate comes from prior midterm election bear years. Bitcoin fell sharply in May 2018 and again in May 2022 before extending lower in June. Jeff Ko’s argument is that those drops were driven by real shocks, not the calendar itself, which makes 2026 a comparison point rather than a rule.\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\nThe immediate risk is that a macro shock keeps building while BTC is already trading below a key support band. If ETF outflows pick up and leveraged selling returns, price could move toward the mid-$60,000s or lower. The bullish counterpoint is that long-term holders remain relatively inactive and exchange balances stay low.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-under-usd77-000-as-oil-shock-and-treasury-yields-hit-risk-ass\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/sell-in-may-historical-pattern-could-see-btc-back-at-33k-analyst?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n- Google News: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixAFBVV95cUxQajIxZExWVjJnMXVteHdZVGVZZFNzbzlvZXVNSFFLaWR2Mm5HekNyVmxrUXpTX0lMMnJfMkJmV1FGdlcwN0U0YVF3U3ZUU2x0ZGhnSG1icE9aM0VSQkJVQldVYnpMN1I1YlhIUXVZTUlTd2NZN2h6VjZieUx5LTFwV0FtRVZQREFCVEZFWTYtU182TFZDTERFcmcySVZqRF9GalRBbXJTR0h2WVVnaHdJa2E2a0dNZ1dURnlpbVpxWERLZkI1?oc=5\n\n[Word Count: XXXX]'''\nwords = text.replace('[Word Count: XXXX]','').split()\nprint(len(words))\nPY"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1663,"output":6313,"cacheRead":15232,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":23208,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779089790812,"responseId":"chatcmpl-Dgmocj8NSCvIIuCR9aaXjVKiLjoGS"}}
