hat backdrop matters because Bitcoin had already been consolidating after a 40% advance. When price is stretched and leveraged positioning is crowded, even a modest external shock can push BTC through short-term support. CoinDesk’s summary added that long-term holders still appear to be sitting tight and exchange balances remain near six-year lows, but underwater short-term holders leave the market open to more downside.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### Sell in May fears meet real macro stress\n\nThe seasonal debate is louder because 2026 lines up with prior midterm election bear phases. CoinTelegraph noted that Bitcoin fell from nearly $10,000 to about $7,000 in May 2018, then from roughly $40,000 to $28,500 in May 2022 before sliding to $20,000 in June.\n\nBut [CoinTelegraph’s analyst roundup](https://cointelegraph.com/news/sell-in-may-historical-pattern-could-see-btc-back-at-33k-analyst?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) made the cleaner point: the calendar did not cause those crashes. Jeff Ko tied the earlier drawdowns to specific shocks such as the Mt. Gox aftermath, China’s ICO crackdown, Fed tightening, and later the Terra and FTX failures.\n\n- 2018 came with post-Mt. Gox stress and China policy pressure\n- 2022 came with aggressive Fed tightening and crypto-specific blowups\n- 2026 so far looks more like a macro-led risk reduction than a systemic break\n\n### Why the structure still looks different this cycle\n\nKo argued that repeating a 70% to 80% collapse from here is a high bar. Spot ETFs, corporate treasury demand, and progress around the CLARITY Act have widened the buyer base compared with earlier bear markets.\n\nThat does not rule out more downside. Ko said a move into the mid-$60,000s or high-$50,000s could still be defensible if a macro shock intensifies or ETF outflows start cascading. A washout toward $33,000 would likely need something systemic to break.\n\n## Key BTC support levels traders should watch now\n\nFor traders, the debate is less about seasonal slogans and more about whether key BTC support levels can absorb this selling wave. [Google News aggregation](https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixAFBVV95cUxQajIxZExWVjJnMXVteHdZVGVZZFNzbzlvZXVNSFFLaWR2Mm5HekNyVmxrUXpTX0lMMnJfMkJmV1FGdlcwN0U0YVF3U3ZUU2x0ZGhnSG1icE9aM0VSQkJVQldVYnpMN1I1YlhIUXVZTUlTd2NZN2h6VjZieUx5LTFwV0FtRVZQREFCVEZFWTYtU182TFZDTERFcmcySVZqRF9GalRBbXJTR0h2WVVnaHdJa2E2a0dNZ1dURnlpbVpxWERLZkI1?oc=5) showed the same theme across outlets, from altcoin weakness to a Bloomberg headline saying crypto liquidations topped half a billion dollars.\n\nThat matters because heavy liquidation days can distort price. A fast flush can rebound hard, but a market that keeps losing support after liquidations usually signals deeper risk reduction is still underway.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### $76,000 needs to hold\n\nMichaël van de Poppe flagged the $76,000 zone as the level currently preventing a larger breakdown. If buyers defend it, BTC can remain in a post-rally consolidation range. If that floor gives way on strong volume, traders should expect a faster move toward lower liquidity pockets.\n\n### $78,000 remains the short-term reclaim\n\nOn the upside, getting back above $78,000 would help cool the immediate bearish tone because several analysts see sub-$78,000 trading as the point where bears start regaining control. A reclaim would not erase the bitcoin selloff, but it would suggest the move was more flush than trend reversal.\n\n- Look for: ETF flow data and whether outflows accelerate after the break below $77,000\n- Look for: Treasury yield and oil moves, since both are driving risk sentiment across markets\n- Look for: Liquidation totals and altcoin relative weakness, which often signal whether forced selling is ending\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe bitcoin selloff has pushed BTC under $77,000, with $76,000 now acting as the line traders cannot afford to ignore.\n\nNear-term structure still points to a correction inside a broader range, not proof of a full cycle collapse. That changes quickly if macro pressure deepens and ETF flows turn decisively negative.\n\nWatch the $76,000 support, the $78,000 reclaim zone, and cross-market signals from yields and oil. If those inputs stabilize, BTC can base. If they worsen, stay defensive and respect the trend.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did the bitcoin selloff happen?**\nBitcoin fell as a macro risk-off move hit speculative assets. CoinDesk tied the drop to an oil shock and higher Treasury yields, while liquidations amplified the decline after BTC slipped through short-term support. Once price moved into the $76,000 to $77,000 zone, leveraged traders started getting forced out.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger correction?**\nIt could become one, but the current setup does not guarantee a full breakdown. Michaël van de Poppe said the chart still looks like consolidation after a 40% run. The bigger warning sign would be a clean loss of $76,000 with worsening macro conditions.\n\n**3. What levels or signals should traders watch now?**\nThe first level is $76,000, which is the nearby support zone analysts are tracking. The next is $78,000, because reclaiming it would weaken the immediate bearish case. Beyond price, watch ETF flows, liquidation totals, and whether oil and Treasury yields keep tightening conditions for risk assets.\n\n**4. How does this affect the broader crypto market?**\nBitcoin rarely drops alone during a macro-driven move, and this session was no exception. CoinDesk-linked coverage showed SOL, DOGE, and XRP also down about 5%, while Bloomberg’s feed highlighted more than half a billion dollars in liquidations across crypto. That kind of selling usually pressures high-beta altcoins harder than BTC.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\nThe sell in May debate comes from prior midterm election bear years. Bitcoin fell sharply in May 2018 and again in May 2022 before extending lower in June. Jeff Ko’s point is that those drops were driven by real shocks, not the calendar itself, which makes 2026 a comparison point rather than a rule.\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\nThe immediate risk is that a macro shock keeps building while BTC is already trading below a key support band. If ETF outflows pick up and leveraged selling returns, price could move toward the mid-$60,000s or lower. The bullish counterpoint is that long-term holders remain relatively inactive and exchange balances stay low.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-under-usd77-000-as-oil-shock-and-treasury-yields-hit-risk-ass\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/sell-in-may-historical-pattern-could-see-btc-back-at-33k-analyst?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n- Google News: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixAFBVV95cUxQajIxZExWVjJnMXVteHdZVGVZZFNzbzlvZXVNSFFLaWR2Mm5HekNyVmxrUXpTX0lMMnJfMkJmV1FGdlcwN0U0YVF3U3ZUU2x0ZGhnSG1icE9aM0VSQkJVQldVYnpMN1I1YlhIUXVZTUlTd2NZN2h6VjZieUx5LTFwV0FtRVZQREFCVEZFWTYtU182TFZDTERFcmcySVZqRF9GalRBbXJTR0h2WVVnaHdJa2E2a0dNZ1dURnlpbVpxWERLZkI1?oc=5\n'''\nprint(len(text.split()))\nPY"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":2452,"output":2810,"cacheRead":26752,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":32014,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779090031579,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgmsVi7KC1XFVL039bINIZ4A2WWn0"}}
